  =========================================================
   OVERVIEW -  Margin Collapsing

   Margin collapsing is a CSS behavior where vertical margins
   (top and bottom) of elements that touch each other merge
   into a single margin. Instead of adding, the browser
   applies only the largest margin value.

   It occurs between:
   - Adjacent sibling elements
   - Parent and first/last child (when no border, padding,
     or inline content exists)
   - Empty block elements

   NOTE:
   - Only vertical margins collapse
   - Horizontal margins never collapse
   ========================================================= 

.box1 {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
}

.box2 {
    margin-top: 20px;
}

.parent {
    margin-top: 30px;
}

.child {
    margin-top: 15px;
}

.empty-box {
    margin-top: 25px;
    margin-bottom: 25px;
}